        "Marital Status: No real effect on spend per transaction — unmarried customers just transact more often.\n",
        "Occupation: Code 4 leads in total revenue (volume); code 17 leads in average spend per transaction.\n",
        "\n",
        "Location\n",
        "\n",
        "City Category: City B leads in total revenue (volume); City C has the highest average spend per transaction.\n",
        "Years in city: No meaningful effect on spending — ruled out as a useful segment.\n",
        "\n",
        "Product\n",
        "\n",
        "Popularity vs Revenue mismatch: Category 5 is bought most often, but Category 1 earns the most total revenue — a \"cheap & popular\" vs \"fewer but pricier\" split.\n",
        "Purchase distribution: Moderately right-skewed (mean > median, skew 0.60) — most transactions cluster ₹5,800–12,000, with a smaller high-spend tail pulling the average up.\n",
        "\n",
        "Customer-level\n",
        "\n",
        "Top spender: Customer 1004277, ₹1.05 crore total — a clear standalone outlier.\n",
        "Biggest single finding of the project: Males aged 26-35 alone drive 31% of total company revenue — more than any other 3-4 segments combined.\n",
        "\n",
        "Cross-analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "City C spends most across nearly every occupation except Occupation 8, which spends most in City A — a real anomaly.\n",
        "Correlation check confirms numerically what the groupbys showed: Occupation (+0.02) and Marital Status (~0.00) have essentially no relationship with Purchase; Product_Category_1 (-0.34) and Product_Category_3 (+0.29) show moderate relationships, though these reflect category codes, not real quantities.\n",
